{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1792454809375,"sessionID":"ses_ea95fb574ffeu0UL1viRR0U87M","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"websearch","callID":"call_00_o8EhsMmGpcEkg0Ji0qKW8308","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"numResults":8,"query":"\"50 million surrogate model evaluations\" \"Keating\" DREAM 241-parameter groundwater Nevada"},"output":"{\n  \"search_id\": \"search_cfd74a455a1ff344068e28e468dc6df3\",\n  \"results\": [\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://pubs.usgs.gov/sir/2023/5008/sir20235008.pdf\",\n      \"title\": \"SIR 2023-5008: Assessing Potential Effects of Changes in Water Use in the Middle Carson River Basin with a Numerical Groundwater-Flow Model, Eagle, Dayton, and Churchill Valleys, West-Central Nevada\",\n      \"publish_date\": \"2023-05-10\",\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"between pumping increased percentages of permitted groundwater rights and the baseline scenario at the end of the 2000–10 simulation period in western Churchill Valley, Nevada, for groundwater pumping increased 25 percent; and groundwater pumping increased 100 percent .......................................................................95\\n50.\\nSummary of active MODFLOW packages in the three simulated valleys .........................34\\n9. List of MODFLOW parameters calibrated by parameter-estimation software in\\nthe middle Carson River model .................................................................................................35\\n10.\\nDescription of the observation group names appearing in the automated\\nparameter-estimation control file ............................................................................................37\\n11. Groundwater monitoring wells used in calibration of the middle Carson\\nThe model can be used to evaluate the effects of proposed alternative management strategies on groundwater sustainability, flows in the Carson River, and routine operation of Lahontan Reservoir and can also provide a basis for basin-wide investigations seeking to quantitatively evaluate the effects of climate change or yet-to-be-determined\\nalternative management strategies.\\nThe middle Carson model was constructed using\\nthe U.S. Geological Survey groundwater modeling software MODFLOW-NWT. MODFLOW is widely used groundwater modeling software and is well-suited for evaluating groundwater and surface-water interactions.\\nAll available observations for this period were used during the model calibration process, performed using automated parameter-estimation software.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://www.usgs.gov/centers/nevada-water-science-center/data\",\n      \"title\": \"Nevada Water Science Center - Data and Tools | U.S. Geological Survey\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"MODFLOW 6 groundwater flow models and supporting data for evaluation of stream capture related to groundwater pumping, middle Humboldt River Basin, Nevada MODFLOW 6 groundwater flow models and supporting data for evaluation of stream capture related to groundwater pumping, middle Humboldt River Basin, Nevada\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://pubs.usgs.gov/sir/2014/5213/pdf/sir2014-5213.pdf\",\n      \"title\": \"Steady-State Numerical Groundwater Flow Model of the Great Basin Carbonate and Alluvial Aquifer System\",\n      \"publish_date\": \"2014-12-10\",\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"38 Influence Statistics .................................................................................................................. 38 Evaluation of Parameter Estimates ....................................................................................... 39\\n64 Horizontal-Flow Barriers ......................................................................................................... 64\\nModel Evaluation ........................................................................................................................................ 64\\nModel Results ..................................................................................................................... 104 Appendix 4. Hydrogeologic Zones, Model Parameter Description, and Model Parameter\\nunit in the numerical groundwater flow model, Great Basin carbonate and alluvial aquifer system study area ........................................................................................................ 50\\n26. Map showing distribution of hydraulic conductivity of the thrusted non-carbonate\\nThis report describes the construction, calibration, evalu-\\nation, and results of a steady-state numerical groundwater flow model of the Great Basin carbonate and alluvial aquifer system that was developed as part of the U.S. Geological Sur- vey National Water Census Initiative to evaluate the nation’s groundwater availability.\\n2 Steady-State Numerical Groundwater Flow Model of the Great Basin Carbonate and Alluvial Aquifer System\\n1 50 389 200 250 300 150 100 350 1\\n50\\n100\\n450\\n500\\n509\\n250\\n200\\n150\\n350\\n400\\n300\\nWinnemucca\\nElko\\nLogan\\nSalt Lake City\\nBaker\\nBeaver\\nCedar City\\nSt George\\nLas Vegas\\n36°\\n42°\\n114°\\n111° 117°\\nCALIFORNIA\\nARIZONA\\nUTAH\\nNEVADA\\nOREGON IDAHO\\nModel Column\\n0 25 50 75 100 Kilometers\\n0 25 50 75 100 Miles\\nCALIFORNIA\\nARIZONA\\nUTAH\\nNEVADA\\nIDAHO\\nRASA-Great Basin Carbonate-Rock Province Regional Aquifer System Analysis model area (Prudic and others, 1995)\\nDeath Valley Regional Flow System model area (Belcher, 2004)\\nBasin and Range Carbonate Aquifer System study area (Welch and others, 2007)\\nThe purpose of this report is to describe the construction,\\ncalibration, evaluation, and results of a steady-state numerical groundwater flow model of the GBCAAS study area.\\nevaluation of existing groundwater monitoring networks, and (5) new approaches for regional groundwater analysis.\\nThe model presented in this report represents steady-state\\nies of eastern Nevada have been completed as part of water- rights applications associated with proposed groundwater development by regional water agencies in southern Nevada (Dixon and others, 2007; Southern Nevada Water Authority and U.S. Bureau of Land Management, 2008; Rowley and others, 2009).\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://independent.academia.edu/KeatingElizabeth\",\n      \"title\": \"Elizabeth Keating - Research Profile\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"Leakage uncertainty was based on hypothetical injection of CO 2 for 50 years at a rate of 5 million tons per year into a depleted oil/gas reservoir with high permeability and, one or more wells provided leakage pathways from the storage reservoir to the overlying aquifer.\\nformat_quote\\ndownload Download free PDF\\nResearch paper thumbnail of Probabilistic evaluation of shallow groundwater resources at a hypothetical carbon sequestration site\\nProbabilistic evaluation of shallow groundwater resources at a hypothetical carbon sequestration site\\nby Diana Bacon , Elizabeth Keating , and Zhenxue Dai\\nScientific Reports , 2014\\nDeveloping a robust geochemical and reactive transport model to evaluate possible sources of arsenic at the CO2 sequestration natural analog site in Chimayo, New Mexico\\nby Hari Viswanathan , Zhenxue Dai , Elizabeth Keating , and Kirk G Scheckel\\nInternational Journal of Greenhouse Gas Control , 2012\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/35088275\",\n      \"title\": \"A construction strategy for conservative adaptive Kriging surrogate model with application in the optimal design of contaminated groundwater extraction-treatment.\",\n      \"publish_date\": \"2022-01-28\",\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"A construction strategy for conservative adaptive Kriging surrogate model with application in the optimal design of contaminated groundwater extraction-treatment.\\nWhen the simulation-optimization model to optimize the groundwater extraction-treatment schemes is used, the construction of a surrogate model for the numerical simulation model has become an effective means to overcome the large calculation load of repeatedly calling the numerical model.\\nIn this paper, a conservative adaptive Kriging surrogate model (CAKSM) was proposed by coupling the Kriging surrogate model, optimal solution adaptive sampling method (OSAS), and conservative prediction idea. Firstly, an initial Kriging surrogate model (IKSM) was built for the numerical simulation model of groundwater flow and solute transport.\\nKeywords\\nGroundwater extraction-treatment; Kriging surrogate model; Optimal solution adaptive sampling method; Safety margin; Simulation-optimization model; Spatial variability of hydraulic conductivity\\nMeSH terms (* = major topic)\\n* Groundwater*\\n* Humans\\n* Models, Theoretical\\n* Reproducibility of Results\\n* Sleep Apnea, Obstructive*\\n* Spatial Analysis\\n  Source: PubMed, U.S. National Library of Medicine.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2404.00053\",\n      \"title\": \"Adaptive Computing for Scale-up Problems\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"Olga Doronina is a Data Scientist at NREL at Golden, CO, 80401, USA. Her research interests in- clude scientific machine learning, surrogate modeling and sensitivity analysis.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://www.osti.gov/servlets/purl/1820582\",\n      \"title\": \"High-Resolution Hourly Surrogate Modeling Framework for ...\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"Surrogate modeling is a broad research field that extends well beyond the building simulation community, and many of its strengths and weaknesses are under\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://science.uptrusthq.com/paper/8c66677f01ac652c8a86b6e5523ff96850e53bbc\",\n      \"title\": \"Optimization and uncertainty assessment of strongly nonlinear… — Sci-Trust\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"Sci-Trust Science corpus · trust-algo showcase\\nBrowse Subfields Compare About\\nBrowse / Paper\\n2010\\nOptimization and uncertainty assessment of strongly nonlinear groundwater models with high parameter dimensionality\\nEfficient posterior exploration of a high‐dimensional groundwater model from two‐stage Markov chain Monte Carlo simulation and polynomial chaos expansion\\nEric Laloy , B. Rogiers , Jasper A. Vrugt , Dirk Mallants , Diederik Jacques · 2013\\nWhile these methods have been successfully implemented for calibrating hydrology models, in many cases they also suffer from difficulties such as inadequately exploring the parameter space [Duan et al., 1992; Bates and Campbell, 2001; Liu and Gupta, 2007; Keating et al., 2010].\\nFor parameter optimization, Bayesian‐based methods have gained considerable attention from the environmental modeling community because they provide a powerful tool from which inferences can be drawn (Clark & Gelfand, 2006; Huisman et al., 2010; Keating et al., 2010; Zhu et al., 2014).\\nAn adaptive sparse‐grid high‐order stochastic collocation method for Bayesian inference in groundwater reactive transport modeling\\nGuannan Zhang , Dan Lu , Ming Ye , Max D. Gunzburger , Clayton G. Webster · 2013\\nOne of the most popular and robust sampling techniques is the Markov Chain Monte Carlo (MCMC) method [Marshall et al., 2005; Gamerman and Lopes, 2006; Vrugt et al., 2008, 2009; Keating et al., 2010; Liu et al., 2010].\\nNeutral Referenced without endorsement\\n5\\nReview of surrogate modeling in water resources\\nSaman Razavi , Bryan Tolson , Donald H.\\nBurn · 2012\\nModern simulation models tend to be computationally intensive as they rigorously represent detailed scientific knowledge about the real-world systems [Keating et al., 2010; Mugunthan et al., 2005; Zhang et al., 2009].\\nMarkov chain Monte Carlo simulation using the DREAM software package: Theory, concepts, and MATLAB implementation\\nJasper A. Vrugt · Environmental Modelling & Software 2016\\n, 2011), hydrologeology (Keating et al., 2010; Laloy et al., 2013; Malama et al., 2013), hydrology (Vrugt et al.\\nGlobal sensitivity analysis in hydrological modeling: Review of concepts, methods, theoretical framework, and applications\\nXiaomeng Song , Jianyun Zhang , Che-sheng Zhan , Yunqing Xuan , Ming Ye , Chonggang Xu · 2015\\nModel calibration or parameter optimization is challenging for complex models due to the uncertainty of a large number of parameters (Fienen et al., 2009; Foglia et al., 2009; Keating et al., 2010; Ye et al., 2014).\\nPhysically based modeling in catchment hydrology at 50: Survey and outlook\\nClaudio Paniconi , Mario Putti · 2015\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://pubs.usgs.gov/pp/1711\",\n      \"title\": \"USGS Professional Paper 1711: Death Valley Regional Groundwater Flow System, Nevada and California—Hydrogeologic Framework and Transient Groundwater Flow Model\",\n      \"publish_date\": \"2010-08-13\",\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"Death Valley Regional Groundwater Flow System, Nevada and California—Hydrogeologic Framework and Transient Groundwater Flow Model\\nEdited by Wayne R. Belcher and Donald S. Sweetkind\\nThumbnail of publication and link to report Front Matter (5 MB)\\nA numerical three-dimensional (3D) transient groundwater flow model of the Death Valley region was developed by the U.S. Geological Survey for the U.S. Department of Energy programs at the Nevada Test Site and at Yucca Mountain, Nevada.\\nGroundwater also is withdrawn for agricultural, commercial, and domestic uses.\\nGroundwater flow in the DVRFS was simulated using MODFLOW-2000, the U.S. Geological Survey 3D finitedifference modular groundwater flow modeling code that incorporates a nonlinear least-squares regression technique to estimate aquifer parameters.\\nThe representation of detail provided by the 3D digital hydrogeologic framework model and the numerical groundwater flow model enabled greater spatial accuracy in every model parameter.\\nTransient Numerical Model\\n* Appendix 1 PDF (602 kB)\\nRegional Potential for Interbasin Flow of Groundwater\\n* Appendix 2 PDF (2.6 MB)\\nEstimated Model Boundary Flows\\n* Plate 1 PDF (9.5 MB)\\nMap showing regional potential for interbasin flow of groundwater in the Death Valley regional groundwater flow system area, Nevada and California.\\n* Plate 2 PDF (17.5 MB)\\nMap showing simulated groundwater response to pumping in the Death Valley regional groundwater flow system area, Nevada and California.\\n* Geospatial data sets\\nSearch on Death Valley regional flow system\\n* Model Archive\\nFor additional information contact:\\nSuggested citation:\\nBelcher, W.R., and Sweetkind, D.S., eds., 2010, Death Valley regional groundwater flow system, Nevada and California—Hydrogeologic framework and transient groundwater flow model: U.S. Geological Survey Professional Paper 1711, 398 p.\\nContents\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://pangea.stanford.edu/ERE/db/GeoConf/papers/SGW/2026/Liang.pdf\",\n      \"title\": \"pangea.stanford.edu\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"PROCEEDINGS, 51 st Workshop on Geothermal Reservoir Engineering Stanford University, Stanford, California, February 9-11, 2026 SGP-TR-230 1 **Surrogate Modeling for Geothermal Systems: Accelerating Optimization, History Matching,** **and Uncertainty Quantification** Zhouji LIANG, Junjie YU, Robin THIBAUT, Fangning ZHENG, Carl HOILAND, Ahinoam POLLACK george@zanskar.us **Keywords:** Machine Learning; Reservoir Simulation; Artificial Intelligence **ABSTRACT** High-fidelity geothermal reservoir simulators are essential for forecasting subsurface pressure and temperature distributions but are\"\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"warnings\": null,\n  \"metadata\": null,\n  \"session_id\": \"ses_ea95fb574ffeu0UL1viRR0U87M\"\n}","metadata":{"provider":"parallel","truncated":false},"t