ribution\\nplot(output, data = data, predictive = 1)\\nfrom a single model\\nplot_trace(output$mod_linear)\\nPosterior Distribution of Minimum X% Effective Dose\\nDescription\\nPosterior Distribution of Minimum X% Effective Dose\\nUsage\\npost_medx(\\nx,\\ned,\\nprobs,\\ntime,\\nlower,\\nupper,\\ngreater,\\nsmall_bound,\\nreturn_samples,\\n...\\n)\\nBayesian model averaging\\npost_medx(output, ed = c(50, 90))\\nsingle model\\npr_medx(output$mod_linear, ed = 80)\\nSummarize Bayesian Model Averaging MCMC Output\\nDescription\\nSummarize parameter inference and convergence diagnostics.\\nUsage\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://eprints.lancs.ac.uk/id/eprint/123541/1/Limits_of_Acceptability_GLUE_DREAM_Resubmitted_Dec_18_2017.pdf\",\n      \"title\": \"Embracing Equifinality with Efficiency: Limits of Acceptability Sampling Using the DREAM algorithm\",\n      \"publish_date\": \"2017-12-20\",\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"1. Draw at random N samples from the prior parameter distribution, P ( x ), and store these\\nrealizations in a N × d matrix X = { x (1) , . . . , x ( N ) } .\\n2. Evaluate the model, y ( i ) ← M ( x ( i ) |· ), and compute the minimum absolute normalised 175\\nscore for the simulation, y ( i ) = { y ( i ) 1 , . . . , y ( i ) n } , to be acceptable.\\n3.\\nthe degree of extension remains necessarily subjective, and possibly guided by the requirements\\nof an application in evaluating model simulations as fit-for-purpose ( Beven , 2017). This may\\npurport a methodological flaw, yet is the consequence of the inexact nature of hydrological\\nscience. 225\\nwork is that ABC assumes use of a stochastic model so that repeated runs with the exact same\\n10\\nparameter values will produce a range of possible simulations. Otherwise, the posterior distri- 335\\nbution, P ( x | ρ  y , y ( x ∗ )  ≤ ϵ ) cannot converge to a stable distribution in the limit of ϵ → 0, and\\nevaluations are required to satisfy the convergence threshold of  R ≤ 1 . 2. The acceptance rate of\\nproposals is equivalent to about 33% (not shown herein), which means that, on average, every\\nthird proposal of DREAM(LOA) satisfies the Limits of Acceptability. This acceptance rate would\\nIn this trivial example it is quite easy to do generate many millions of samples from the uni-\\nform prior distribution to compensate for a poor sampling efficiency, nevertheless, the prospects 455\\nfor much higher dimensional search spaces with much more complex parameter interactions\\nare not very encouraging.\\nlower than DREAM(LOA), and expected to deteriorate further with increasing dimensionality\\nand size of the parameter space.\\nTo provide further insights into the convergence speed of DREAM(LOA), Figure 11 plots the\\nevolution of the  R -diagnostic for the six HYDRUS-1D model parameters in the top panel and 635\\ntraces of the sampled fitness values of the N = 8 different chains simulated with DREAM(LOA) in the bottom panel. The  R -diagnostic of Gelman and Rubin (1992) satisfies the convergence\\nthreshold (black line) after about 4 , 800 function evaluations. This means that the last 50% of the\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://hess.copernicus.org/preprints/8/4025/2011/hessd-8-4025-2011.pdf\",\n      \"title\": \"DREAM(D)  discrete MCMC simulation\",\n      \"publish_date\": \"2011-04-26\",\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"schemes to the appropriate limiting distribution under a variety of different conditions. 5\\nIn practice, this convergence is often observed to be impractically slow. This deficiency is frequently caused by an inappropriate selection of q (·) used to generate trial moves in the Markov Chain. This inspired Vrugt et al.\\nIn previous papers, we have given formal proofs of convergence of DREAM (Vrugt\\net al., 2008), DREAM(ZS) (Vrugt et al., 2011), and MT-DREAM(ZS) (Laloy and Vrugt, 2011) to the appropriate limiting distribution. For simplicity, we use a hypothetical ex- ample to illustrate that DREAM(D) maintains detailed balance.\\n4c is a further refinement, but with a few noticeable deviations from the true solution. Finally, after about 1 million sudoku function evaluations, the puzzle is successfully solved (Fig. 4d). 15\\nObviously, it is inspiring that DREAM(D) is able to successfully solve a Sudoku puzzle,\\nThe convergence speed for both algorithms is strikingly similar. Both MCMC methods need about 30 000 model evaluations to converge to a stationary (limiting) distribution (indicated with the dashed blue horizontal line). Although gridding significantly reduces the size of the\\n4039\\nfeasible parameter space, this has no immediate effect on sampling efficiency. An approximately similar number of model runs is required to converge to and explore the posterior target distribution. Yet, this by no means is a universal finding.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://agupubs.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1002/2014WR015386\",\n      \"title\": \"Approximate Bayesian Computation using Markov Chain Monte Carlo simulation: DREAM<sub>(ABC)</sub> - Sadegh - 2014 - Water Resources Research - Wiley Online Library\",\n      \"publish_date\": \"2014-08-01\",\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"In the limit of urn:x-wiley:00431397:media:wrcr21058:wrcr21058-math-0022 the sampled distribution will converge to the true posterior, urn:x-wiley:00431397:media:wrcr21058:wrcr21058-math-0023 [ Pritchard et al ., 1999 ; Beaumont et al ., 2002 ; Ratmann et al ., 2009 ; Turner and van Zandt , 2012 ].\\nConceptual overview of approximate Bayesian computation (ABC) for a hypothetical one-dimensional parameter estimation problem. First, N samples are drawn from a user-defined prior distribution, urn:x-wiley:00431397:media:wrcr21058:wrcr21058-math-0025 . Then, this ensemble is evaluated with the model and creates N model simulations.\\nTo guarantee convergence to the appropriate limiting distribution, the value of ϵ in Algorithm 1 (ABC-REJ) needs to be taken very small. Values of urn:x-wiley:00431397:media:wrcr21058:wrcr21058-math-0054 are often deemed appropriate.\\nThe ABC-PMC sampler on the other hand requires fewer function evaluations (170,848) to explore the target distribution, with an acceptance rate of about 0.59%.\\nThe binary acceptance probability of equation 13 differs fundamentally from a regular MCMC selection rule, but has important practical advantages that promulgate converge to the correct limiting distribution.\\nWe now benchmark the performance of DREAM (ABC) by application to the Gaussian mixture model in equation 5 . We set urn:x-wiley:00431397:media:wrcr21058:wrcr21058-math-0215 and run K = 10 different chains using a total of M = 50, 000 function evaluations. Figure 2 c displays the marginal distribution of the posterior samples using a burn-in of 50%.\\nThe present case study clearly illustrates the advantages of DREAM (ABC) when confronted with multidimensional parameter spaces. The algorithm requires about M = 200, 000 function evaluations to successfully recover the 20-D target distribution.\\nValues of urn:x-wiley:00431397:media:wrcr21058:wrcr21058-math-0232 smaller than 1.2 indicate convergence to a limiting distribution. The DREAM (ABC) algorithm needs about 40, 000 function evaluations to officially reach convergence and generate a sufficient sample of the posterior distribution.\\nObviously, one should be careful to judge convergence of the sampled Markov chains based on a single diagnostic, yet visual inspection of the sampled trajectories confirms an adequate mixing of the different chains and convergence of DREAM (ABC) after about 15, 000 function evaluations.\\nTrace plots of the urn:x-wiley:00431397:media:wrcr21058:wrcr21058-math-0234 -statistic of the sampled Markov chains with DREAM (ABC) for the 20-dimensional bivariate normal distribution. Each parameter is coded with a different color. The dashed line denotes the default threshold used to diagnose convergence to a limiting distribution.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0021999110004699\",\n      \"title\": \"Evaluation of failure probability via surrogate models - ScienceDirect\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"Abstract Evaluation of failure probability of a given system requires sampling of the system response and can be computationally expensive. Therefore it is desirable to construct an accurate surrogate model for the system response and subsequently to sample the surrogate model. In this paper we discuss the properties of this approach. We demonstrate that the straightforward sampling of a surrogate model can lead to erroneous results, no matter how accurate the surrogate model is. We then propose a hybrid approach by sampling both the surrogate model in a \\\"large\\\" portion of the probability space and the original system in a \\\"small\\\" portion. The resulting algorithm is significantly more efficient than the traditional sampling method, and is more accurate and robust than the straightforward surrogate model approach. Rigorous convergence proof is established for the hybrid approach, and practical implementation is discussed. Numerical examples are provided to verify the theoretical findings and demonstrate the efficiency gain of the approach.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://rdrr.io/rforge/dream/man/dream.html\",\n      \"title\": \"dream: DiffeRential Evolution Adaptive Metropolis (DREAM) in dream: DiffeRential Evolution Adaptive Metropolis\",\n      \"publish_date\": \"2016-03-16\",\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"These are not implemented in this package. Please read the following references for details.\\nVrugt, J. A. and Ter Braak, C. J. F. (2011) DREAM(D): an adaptive Markov Chain Monte Carlo simulation algorithm to solve discrete, noncontinuous, and combinatorial posterior parameter estimation problems, Hydrol. Earth Syst. Sci.\\nArguments\\n|`FUN` |model function with first argument a vector of parameter values of\\nlength ndim. |\\n| --- | --- |\\n|`func.type` |type of value `FUN` returns. One of:\\nArguments\\n|`INIT.pars` |a list of any extra arguments to be passed to the `INIT` function. |\\n|`control` |list of settings for the DREAM algorithm. see below. |\\n|`measurement` |Required parameter for `func.type` s starting with `calc` .\\nA list with element `data` , a vector containing observed\\ndependent variable values. |\\nDetails\\n| _Element_ | _Default_ | _Description_ |\\n| values outside of parameter bounds. |  |  |\\n| One of: \\\"reflect\\\", \\\"bound\\\", \\\"fold\\\", \\\"none\\\",\\\"rand\\\" |  |  |\\n| `burnin.length` | 0.1 | If `<1` proportion of |\\n| function evaluations to use as burn-in period. If `>1` number |  |  |\\n| of function evaluations to use. |  |  |\\nDetails\\n| _Element_ | _Default_ | _Description_ |\\n| `REPORT` | 1000 | Approximate number of function evaluations |\\n| between calculation and reporting of convergence diagnostics. 0 if |  |  |\\n| no reporting or calculation is desired. |  |  |\\n| Frequent calculation is likely to slow performance. The summary |  |  |\\nDetails\\n| _Element_ | _Default_ | _Description_ |\\n| `ndraw` | 1e5 | Maximum number of function evaluations. May |\\n| terminate before convergence. |  |  |\\n| `maxtime` | Inf | Maximum duration of optimization in seconds. May |\\n| terminate before convergence. |  |  |\\n| `Rthres` | 1.01 | Value of Gelman & Rubin's convergence |\\nDetails\\nDefault settings may not be sufficient for model to run. For few\\nparameters, nseq may need to be increased.\\nIf dream frequently warns that it is reentering the burn-period, `control$burnin.length` may need to be increased.\\nValue\\n| `X` | converged nseq points in parameter space. matrix `nseq x ndim` . |\\n| chains to the posterior distribution. (N.B. in nonlinear |  |\\n| optimisation, this is not a measure of convergence to an optimum parameter set) |  |\\n| `CR` | Probability of crossover at each step. matrix `n.elem/steps x |\\n| 1+length(pCR)` . |  |\\nValue\\n| `X` | converged nseq points in parameter space. matrix `nseq x ndim` . |\\n| `in.burnin` | Boolean showing whether dream was in the burn-in |\\n| period at termination (see description above). |  |\\n| `fun.evals` | Total number of function evaluations. |\\n| `time` | running time (wall time) in seconds. |\\nSee Also\\n* `demo(example1)` Fitting a banana shaped distribution - as in the original matlab code\\n* `demo(example2)` Fitting an n-dimensional Gaussian distribution as\\n  in the original matlab code\\n[dream documentation](/rforge/dream/) built on May 2, 2019, 5:21 p.m.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://escholarship.org/uc/item/91m9t1h3\",\n      \"title\": \"DREAM(D): an adaptive Markov Chain Monte Carlo simulation algorithm to solve discrete, noncontinuous, and combinatorial posterior parameter estimation problems\",\n      \"publish_date\": \"2011-12-13\",\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"UC Irvine UC Irvine Previously Published Works banner UC Irvine\\n+ [PDF](/content/qt91m9t1h3/qt91m9t1h3.pdf)\\n  Share\\n  Email Facebook\\nDREAM(D): an adaptive Markov Chain Monte Carlo simulation algorithm to solve discrete, noncontinuous, and combinatorial posterior parameter estimation problems\\n2011\\n* Vrugt, J. A ;\\n* Ter Braak, C. J. F\\nUC Irvine UC Irvine Previously Published Works banner UC Irvine\\nFormal and informal Bayesian approaches have found widespread implementation and use in environmental modeling to summarize parameter and predictive uncertainty.\\nUC Irvine UC Irvine Previously Published Works banner UC Irvine\\nSuccessful implementation of these methods relies heavily on the availability of efficient sampling methods that approximate, as closely and consistently as possible the (evolving) posterior target distribution.\\nUC Irvine UC Irvine Previously Published Works banner UC Irvine\\nMuch of this work has focused on continuous variables that can take on any value within their prior defined ranges. Here, we introduce theory and concepts of a discrete sampling method that resolves the parameter space at fixed points.\\nUC Irvine UC Irvine Previously Published Works banner UC Irvine\\nThis new code, entitled DREAM((D)) uses the recently developed DREAM algorithm (Vrugt et al., 2008, 2009a,b) as its main building block but implements two novel proposal distributions to help solve discrete and combinatorial optimization problems.\\nUC Irvine UC Irvine Previously Published Works banner UC Irvine\\nThis novel MCMC sampler maintains detailed balance and ergodicity, and is especially designed to resolve the emerging class of optimal experimental design problems.\\nUC Irvine UC Irvine Previously Published Works banner UC Irvine\\nThree different case studies involving a Sudoku puzzle, soil water retention curve, and rainfall - runoff model calibration problem are used to benchmark the performance of DREAM((D)).\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://cran.r-project.org/web/packages/dreamer/vignettes/dreamer_method.html\",\n      \"title\": \"dreamer Methods - CRAN\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"In cases where (p(y \\\\mid m)) is difficult to compute, Gould (2019) proposed using the observed data’s fit to the posterior predictive distribution as a surrogate in calculating the posterior weights; this is the approach used by dreamer.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://cran.r-project.org/web//packages/dreamer/index.html\",\n      \"title\": \"CRAN: Package dreamer\",\n      \"publish_date\": \"2024-12-19\",\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"dreamer: Dose Response Models for Bayesian Model Averaging\\nFits dose-response models utilizing a Bayesian model averaging approach as outlined in Gould (2019) < doi:10.1002/bimj.201700211 > for both continuous and binary responses.\\nLongitudinal dose-response modeling is also supported in a Bayesian model averaging framework as outlined in Payne, Ray, and Thomann (2024) < doi:10.1080/10543406.2023.2292214 >. Functions for plotting and calculating various posterior quantities (e.g. posterior mean, quantiles, probability of minimum efficacious dose, etc.) are also implemented.\"\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"warnings\": null,\n  \"metadata\": null,\n  \"session_id\": \"ses_ea95fb574ffeu0UL1viRR0U87M\"\n}","metadata":{"provider":"parallel","truncated":false},"title":"Parallel Web Search: \"DREAM required about 50 million\" surrogate model evaluations converge limiting parameter distribution","time":{"start":1792454815123,"end":1792454815712}},"id":"prt_156a1ec84001m6HAaQOXbon369","sessionID":"ses_ea95fb574ffeu0UL1viRR0U87M","messageID":"msg_156a1d84c001Ed62qBEA5HdA5S"}}
