2012 · Cited by 663 — In this paper, we present a general framework for efficient inversion of highly parameterized models. This method, entitled MT-DREAM(ZS),\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://www.semanticscholar.org/author/E.-Laloy/5346056\",\n      \"title\": \"E. Laloy | Semantic Scholar\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"High‐dimensional posterior exploration of hydrologic models using multiple‐try DREAM(ZS) and high‐performance computing\\nE. Laloy J. Vrugt\\nEnvironmental Science, Computer Science\\nHigh‐dimensional posterior exploration of hydrologic models using multiple‐try DREAM(ZS) and high‐performance computing\\n* 1 January 2012\\nTLDR\\nHigh‐dimensional posterior exploration of hydrologic models using multiple‐try DREAM(ZS) and high‐performance computing\\nMT‐DREAM(ZS), which combines the strengths of multiple‐try sampling, snooker updating, and sampling from an archive of past states is introduced, which is especially designed to solve high‐dimensional search problems and\\nHigh‐dimensional posterior exploration of hydrologic models using multiple‐try DREAM(ZS) and high‐performance computing\\nreceives particularly spectacular performance improvement over other adaptive MCMC approaches when using distributed computing. Expand\\n* 538\\n* 30\\n* PDF (opens in a new tab)\\nWiley (opens in a new tab)\\nSave\\nAlert\\nCite\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://search.sl.nsw.gov.au/primo-explore/fulldisplay?context=PC&docid=TN_cdi_proquest_journals_1020329913&vid=SLNSW\",\n      \"title\": \"High-dimensional posterior exploration of hydrologic models using multiple-try DREAM(ZS) and high-performance computing | Catalogue | State Library of New South Wales\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"High-dimensional posterior exploration of hydrologic models using multiple-try DREAM(ZS) and high-performance computing\\n: Laloy, Eric ; Vrugt, Jasper A. : : Washington: Blackwell Publishing Ltd :\\nWater resources research, 2012-01, Vol.48 (1), p.n/a\\nPEER REVIEWED\\nOPEN ACCESS\\nGet PDF Read Online\\nnui.aria.newWindow High-dimensional posterior exploration of hydrologic models using multiple-try DREAM(ZS) and high-performance computing Full text available\\nSend to\\nTitle\\nHigh-dimensional posterior exploration of hydrologic models using multiple-try DREAM(ZS) and high-performance computing\\nHigh-dimensional posterior exploration of hydrologic models using multiple-try DREAM(ZS) and high-performance computing\\nHigh-dimensional posterior exploration of hydrologic models using multiple-try DREAM(ZS) and high-performance computing\\nShow collections Hide collections Show All Show Less\\nAuthor / Creator\\nLaloy, Eric\\nLaloy, Eric\\nLaloy, Eric\\nVrugt, Jasper A.\\nVrugt, Jasper A.\\nVrugt, Jasper A.\\nShow collections Hide collections Show All Show Less\\nContents\\nThe usefulness and applicability of such complex models is increasingly held back by the potentially many hundreds (thousands) of parameters that require calibration against some historical record of data.\\nThe current generation of search and optimization algorithms is typically not powerful enough to deal with a very large number of variables and summarize parameter and predictive uncertainty.\\nWe have previously presented a general‐purpose Markov chain Monte Carlo (MCMC) algorithm for Bayesian inference of the posterior probability density function of hydrologic model parameters.\\nThis method, entitled differential evolution adaptive Metropolis (DREAM), runs multiple different Markov chains in parallel and uses a discrete proposal distribution to evolve the sampler to the posterior distribution. The DREAM approach maintains detailed balance and shows excellent performance on complex, multimodal search problems.\\nHere we present our latest algorithmic developments and introduce MT‐DREAM(ZS), which combines the strengths of multiple‐try sampling, snooker updating, and sampling from an archive of past states.\\nThis new code is especially designed to solve high‐dimensional search problems and receives particularly spectacular performance improvement over other adaptive MCMC approaches when using distributed computing. Four different case studies with increasing dimensionality up to 241 parameters are used to illustrate the advantages of MT‐DREAM(ZS).\\nThis new code is especially designed to solve high‐dimensional search problems and receives particularly spectacular performance improvement over other adaptive MCMC approaches when using distributed computing. Four different case studies with increasing dimensionality up to 241 parameters are used to illustrate the advantages of MT‐DREAM(ZS).\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://agupubs.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1029/2011WR010608\",\n      \"title\": \"High‐dimensional posterior exploration of hydrologic models using ...\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"High‐dimensional posterior exploration of hydrologic models using ...\\nThis method, entitled MT-DREAM(ZS), combines the strengths of differential evolution, subspace exploration, sampling from past states, snooker\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://collection.sl.nsw.gov.au/record/TN_cdi_proquest_journals_1020329913\",\n      \"title\": \"High-dimensional posterior exploration of hydrologic models using multiple-try DREAM(ZS) and high-performance computing | Collection - State Library of NSW\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"High-dimensional posterior exploration of hydrologic models using multiple-try DREAM(ZS) and high-pe...\\nShow all\\nHelp | Ask | Become a Library member\\nHigh-dimensional posterior exploration of hydrologic models using multiple-try DREAM(ZS) and high-pe...\\nShow all\\nhttps://collection.sl.nsw.gov.au/record/TN_cdi_proquest_journals_1020329913\\nHigh-dimensional posterior exploration of hydrologic models using multiple-try DREAM(ZS) and high-performance computing\\nAbout this item\\nFull title\\nHigh-dimensional posterior exploration of hydrologic models using multiple-try DREAM(ZS) and high-performance computing\\nAuthor / Creator\\nLaloy, Eric and Vrugt, Jasper A.\\nPublisher\\nWashington: Blackwell Publishing Ltd\\nJournal title\\nWater resources research, 2012-01, Vol.48 (1), p.n/a\\nLanguage\\nEnglish\\nFormats\\nArticles\\nAlternative Titles\\nFull title\\nHigh-dimensional posterior exploration of hydrologic models using multiple-try DREAM(ZS) and high-performance computing\\nAuthors, Artists and Contributors\\nAuthor / Creator\\nLaloy, Eric Vrugt, Jasper A.\\nIdentifiers\\nPrimary Identifiers\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2601.03532v1\",\n      \"title\": \"Propagating Surrogate Uncertainty in Bayesian Inverse Problems\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"spatial locations [Oliver et al., 1997, Laloy and Vrugt, 2012, Efendiev et al.,\\n32\\n2006]. Challenges of such inverse problems include the infinite dimensionality of the parameter and the high cost of the forward model [Marzouk and Najm, 2009].\\n6.3.1 Experimental Setup\\nMechanistic Model.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://www.papers.phmsociety.org/index.php/phmconf/article/view/2521\",\n      \"title\": \"\\n\\t\\tProbabilistic Prognosis of Non-Planar Fatigue Crack Growth\\n\\t\\t\\t\\t\\t\\t\\t| Annual Conference of the PHM Society\\n\\t\\t\\t\",\n      \"publish_date\": \"2016-10-03\",\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"Ekvall (Eds.), Probabilistic fracture mechanics and fatigue methods: Applications for structural design and maintenance (pp. 42–66). Philadelphia: American Society for Testing and Materials. Laloy, E., & Vrugt, J. A. (2012). High-dimensional posterior exploration of hydrologic models using multipletry DREAM (ZS) and high-performance computing.\\nWater Resources Research, 48(1), 239–249. Leser, P. E., Hochhalter, J. D., Warner, J. E., Newman, J. A., Leser, W. P., Wawrzynek, P. A., & Yuan, F.-G. (2016). IWSHM 2015: Probabilistic fatigue damage prognosis using surrogate models trained via three-dimensional finite element analysis. Structural Health Monitoring (currently online only).\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://gmd.copernicus.org/preprints/gmd-2018-272/gmd-2018-272-AR3.pdf\",\n      \"title\": \"Bold black font: Topical editor and reviewer comments Black font: Author response Topical Editor Decision: Publish subject to technical corrections\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"the soil respiration model parameters using the MT-DREAM(ZS) code (Laloy and Vrugt, 2012). 319\\nMT-DREAM(ZS) implements a Markov chain Monte Carlo (MCMC) algorithm by running 320\\nmultiple Markov chains in parallel with adaptive proposal distribution, multiple-try sampling, and 321\\nsampling from an archive of past states.\\nThese state-of-the-art features assist in overcoming 322\\ncommon challenges in the sampling space such as multimodality, ill-conditioning, and high 323\\ndimensionality, and thus allow for accurate exploration of the targeted distributions. 324\\n2.3 Soil respiration models 325\\nZhang et al.\"\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"warnings\": null,\n  \"metadata\": null,\n  \"session_id\": \"ses_ea95fb574ffeu0UL1viRR0U87M\"\n}","metadata":{"provider":"parallel","truncated":false},"title":"Parallel Web Search: Laloy Vrugt 2012 \"High-dimensional posterior exploration\" MT-DREAM(ZS) pdf 241-dimensional inverse problem abstract","time":{"start":1792454827896,"end":1792454828471}},"id":"prt_156a21e67001Saz65yGCZSkQ6A","sessionID":"ses_ea95fb574ffeu0UL1viRR0U87M","messageID":"msg_156a1f2cd001kFgkTu7uWDaxzM"}}
